+`list` shows individual expenses rather than totals:
 
 ```
 python -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03
+python -m tally.cli list --month 2024-03 --category food
+```
+
+`stats` gives a quick overall summary — total count, total spend, average, and
+the date range covered:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli stats
+```
+
+`export` writes everything out as CSV so you can open it in a spreadsheet after all:
+
+```
 python -m tally.cli export --out march.csv
+python -m tally.cli export            # writes to stdout
+```
+
+## Tracking budgets
+
+Monthly budgets for each category are set in `tally/config.py` under
+`CATEGORY_BUDGETS`. The defaults are a rough starting point — edit them to match
+what you actually want to spend. Once they are set, `budget` shows how the current
+month stacks up:
+
 ```
+python -m tally.cli budget --month 2024-03
+```
+
+The output flags categories that have passed 80 % of their budget so you can see
+trouble coming before the month is over.
+
+## Using multiple stores
+
+By default tally reads and writes `tally.json` in the current directory. Pass
+`--store` anywhere on the command line to use a different file:
+
+```
+python -m tally.cli --store ~/finances/2024.json add 12.50 food
+python -m tally.cli --store ~/finances/2024.json report --month 2024-03
+```
+
+This also works for keeping work and personal expenses separate.
 
 ## How amounts are stored
 
